		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.wrestleview.com/?p=397331</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>WWF Monday Night Raw ResultsNovember 11, 1996 (Taped October 21)Fort Wayne, Indiana (War Memorial Coliseum)Commentary: Vince McMahon and&#8230;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/397331-wwf-raw-results-november-11-1996-fort-wayne/">WWF Raw Results &#8211; 11/11/96 (Final build to Survivor Series, Shawn Michaels and Sycho Sid go for the Tag Team Titles)</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com">WWE News, WWE Results, AEW News, AEW Results</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full is-resized"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="750" height="382" src="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1.png" alt="" class="wp-image-397332" style="aspect-ratio:1.9634483060875663;width:203px;height:auto" srcset="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1.png 750w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1-600x306.png 600w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1-379x193.png 379w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1-550x280.png 550w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 750px) 100vw, 750px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Monday Night Raw Results<br>November 11, 1996 (Taped October 21)<br>Fort Wayne, Indiana (War Memorial Coliseum)<br>Commentary: Vince McMahon and Jerry “The King” Lawler<br>Results by: Mike Tedesco of Wrestleview.com</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“The World Wrestling Federation: for over fifty years, the revolutionary force in sports entertainment.” The WWF video leads us into the show.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> The Raw video plays.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WWF Tag Team Champions The British Bulldog and Owen Hart head to the ring, accompanied by Clarence Mason.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Replay:</strong> <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/395776-wwf-raw-results-october-21-1996-fort-wayne/">Three weeks ago on Raw</a>, Sycho Sid battled Owen Hart, only for The British Bulldog to interfere. WWF Champion Shawn Michaels ran down to make the save, but Sid didn’t appreciate Michaels&#8217;s involvement. <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/396253-wwf-raw-results-october-28-1996-fort-wayne/">Two weeks ago</a>, Michaels battled the Bulldog, but Hart interfered, resulting in a disqualification. Sid ran down for the save and accidentally hit Michaels from behind, angering the WWF Champion. <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/396650-wwf-raw-results-november-4-1996-fort-wayne/">Last week on Raw</a>, Sid and Michaels got into a shoving match before Vader, Hart, and Bulldog attacked. Hart hit Sid with a chair, which Michaels quickly took from him. Sid turned and saw Michaels with the chair. Sid thought Michaels hit him with the weapon.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Locker Room Interview</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Stone Cold” Steve Austin is shown, and Vince McMahon says, “We apologize for what happened last week with Stone Cold. Austin says, “That’s all you ever seem to do, McMahon, is apologize. And, personally, I think it’s pathetic. Stone Cold ain’t gonna apologize for nothing because I’m gonna keep turning the heat on higher and higher.” As far as Bret “Hitman” Hart goes, he knows he’s here. When he’s done with Bob Holly, Austin will find his locker room and put the Sharpshooter on him. They don’t have to wait for Madison Square Garden… and that’s the bottom line!</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706695 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> I spoke in detail about the insane “Pillman’s Got a Gun” angle on last week’s recap, but this is essentially what’s left of the fallout. Over the weekend, Vince McMahon took the fall to protect the USA Network from further criticism, taking responsibility for what happened on WWF Livewire. Tonight, aside from an apology for what happened the week before, the angle is not mentioned again, nor are any clips of it shown. Unlike the last two weeks, there doesn’t appear to be a portion of the show that is live against the taped segments.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706697 wp-block-paragraph">As much as the WWF and Brian Pillman apologized, “Stone Cold” Steve Austin stays true to his character by refusing to apologize and promising to keep cranking up the heat. After last week’s angle, we won’t be seeing Pillman for quite a while.</p>



<h3 id="wwf-tag-team-championshipowen-hart-and-the-british-bulldog-cs-w-clarence-mason-vs-sycho-sid-and-wwf-champion-shawn-michaels-w-jose-lothario" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>WWF Tag Team Championship</strong><br>Owen Hart and The British Bulldog (c’s) w/ Clarence Mason vs. Sycho Sid and WWF Champion Shawn Michaels w/ José Lothario</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Earl Hebner</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Shawn Michaels and Sycho Sid discuss who will start the match. Owen Hart attacks Sid from behind, but Sid absorbs the impact. Sid sends Hart HARD into the turnbuckles sternum-first before slamming him down by his hair. Hart quickly retreats to the corner. Sid punches him in the midsection and sends him across the ring. Hart slingshots over him before hitting some punches, followed by the ten punches. Sid shoves Hart off and wrenches Hart’s arm before tagging in the WWF Champion. Shawn Michaels comes off the top rope with a double ax handle to Hart’s shoulder. Michaels wrenches Hart’s arm before reversing a whip. Hart shoulder tackles him and hits the ropes. Michaels drops down before doing a leapfrog, followed by a monkey flip. Michaels connects with a hip toss before hitting a clothesline over the top rope. Michaels skins the cat as we go to commercial.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Michaels hits The British Bulldog with a hip toss, followed by an arm drag. Sid tags in and hits a running boot to Bulldog’s midsection. Sid kicks away at Bulldog’s midsection before sending him into the ropes, but he lowers his head and eats a kick. Bulldog powers Sid up with a delayed vertical suplex. Bulldog goes for a leg drop, but Sid moves. Sid kicks Bulldog in the midsection and tags Michaels back in. Michaels comes off the second rope with a punch to the ribs. Michaels hits the ropes, but Hart knees him in the lower back. Bulldog headbutts Michaels down and tags Hart in. Hart and Bulldog deliver a wishbone split to the WWF Champion. Hart hits an inverted atomic drop and stomps his midsection several times. Hart pulls Michaels to his feet and punches him in the head. Michaels fights back, but Hart rakes his eyes. Hart puts Michaels in the corner until the referee backs him up. Bulldog chokes Michaels. Sid gets in the ring, so the referee holds him back. Hart and Bulldog double-team Michaels. Hart sends Michaels into the ropes and turns him inside out with a kitchen sink knee to the midsection. Hart locks on a rear chin lock. Michaels fights up and elbows out. Michaels hits the ropes, ducks a clothesline, and counters a kitchen sink with a roll-up for a two-count. Hart quickly grounds Michaels with a clothesline.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bulldog tags in, and he and Hart hit another wishbone split. Bulldog stomps away at Michaels and bounces on his feet. Bulldog bounces Michaels off the top turnbuckle and punches away at him. Bulldog punches Michaels down and tags Hart in. Hart headbutts Michaels and floors him with a dropkick. Hart taunts Sid, so he gets in the ring. The referee holds Sid back, and Hart and Bulldog double-team Michaels. Jerry “The King” Lawler thinks Sid is intentionally letting Michaels get double-teamed. Hart hits a neckbreaker and goes to the top rope for a diving crossbody block. Michaels rolls through and picks up a two-count. Bulldog punches Michaels down and tags in. Bulldog chokes Michaels with his boot until the referee admonishes him. Hart pulls Michaels up by his ear. Sid gets in the ring, and the referee is furious. Bulldog and Hart double-team Michaels with a choke.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Bret “Hitman” Hart says he respects Steve Austin. He is a great wrestler. Austin is a machine. Hart could come back and wrestle anyone, but he wants to wrestle him. Hart says there is nobody who can touch him in the World Wrestling Federation.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Bulldog stomps away at Michaels. Bulldog tags Hart in as he sends Michaels into the ropes. Hart floors Michaels with a vicious spinning heel kick for a near fall. Hart pulls Michaels up and goes for a suplex, but Michaels counters with an inside cradle for a two-count. Bulldog tags in and stomps away at Michaels before covering. The referee is late on the count, and Bulldog picks up a two-count. Shawn Michaels will be live on America Online tomorrow night at 8 p.m. Eastern. Michaels starts to fight back, but Bulldog low-blows him right in front of the referee. Bulldog says it’s unintentional. Sid shoves Bulldog, so the referee admonishes him. Hart and Bulldog double-team Michaels. Hart stays in despite not tagging in. Hart punches Michaels before tagging Bulldog back in. Bulldog holds Michaels up as Hart goes to the top rope. Hart goes for a missile dropkick, but he hits Bulldog when Michaels moves.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sid tags in to a huge ovation. Sid hits Hart with a big boot. Sid sends Bulldog into the ropes. Bulldog ducks a clothesline, but Sid quickly catches him with a chokeslam. Sid sets up for a Powerbomb and hits Hart with a back body drop as he charges. Bulldog gets out of the Powerbomb with a lame back body drop. Michaels punches Hart out of the ring and tunes up the band. Michaels goes for Sweet Chin Music on the Bulldog, but he ducks, and Sid eats the kick! Michaels is not happy. Hart pulls Michaels out of the ring as Bulldog pins Sid!</p>



<h3 id="winners-by-pinfall-and-still-wwf-tag-team-champions-owen-hart-and-the-british-bulldog" class="wp-block-heading">Winners by Pinfall and still WWF Tag Team Champions: Owen Hart and The British Bulldog</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Michaels fights Hart off at ringside before getting in the ring and attacking Bulldog. Hart gets in the ring, but Michaels blocks a kick. Hart then knocks Michaels out with an enzuigiri! Michaels and Sid are out cold in the ring.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706743 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> This was a good tag team match. I would say there was a little too much heel dominance and not enough of a face comeback to make it truly exciting, but Vince McMahon and Jerry “The King” Lawler actually did a great job of enhancing this match with their commentary. Lawler talking about how Sid was intentionally coming into the ring to allow Michaels to be double-teamed, potentially weakening him ahead of their huge WWF Title match on Sunday at the Survivor Series was a great story to tell. Michaels was phenomenal on the sell, as usual. Sid, as he has been for much of this run, was pretty immobile for the little that he did, but the crowd is eating up everything he does. The pop on a long television taping was undeniable. Good drama at the end, with Michaels accidentally hitting Sid with Sweet Chin Music, costing them the match.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> The WWF Superstar Line is open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. On Option 6, Jim Ross will have a scoop on who the mystery partner will be at Survivor Series. Just call 1-900-747-4WWF. It’s just $1.49 a minute! Kids must have their parents’ permission.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Replay:</strong> Moments ago, Shawn Michaels inadvertently hit Sycho Sid with Sweet Chin Music, costing them the WWF Tag Team Championship.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Arena Hallway</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kevin Kelly says it was “sheer pandemonium” moments ago. WWF officials separated Shawn Michaels and Sycho Sid, but he’ll still have interviews with both of them.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Survivor Series Report</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Dok Hendrix is in the WWF Studios with the WWF Survivor Series Report. Hendrix has all the Survivor Series Elimination Matches.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706780 wp-block-paragraph">&#8212; <strong>Survivor Series Elimination Match:</strong> WWF Intercontinental Champion Hunter Hearst Helmsley, Crush, Goldust, and Jerry “The King” Lawler vs. “Wildman” Marc Mero, “The Stalker” Barry Windham, Mark Henry, and Rocky Maivia<br>&#8212; <strong>Survivor Series Elimination Match:</strong> Faarooq, Vader, Razor Ramon, and Diesel vs. Yokozuna, Savio Vega, Flash Funk, and a mystery partner<br>&#8212; <strong>Survivor Series Elimination Match:</strong> The Godwinns, Phil Lafon, and Doug Furnas vs. WWF Tag Team Champions Owen Hart and The British Bulldog and The New Rockers</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Arena Hallway</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kevin Kelly is outside Sycho Sid’s locker room. Kelly says he’ll let Sid cool down, but Sid says he has a lot on his mind. Sounds are heard from inside the locker room, like Sid is punching the door.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mankind makes his way to the ring with Paul Bearer and The Executioner.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WWF Monday Night Raw is brought to you by Levi’s, Lego Mania, and Peanut M&amp;Ms.</p>



<h3 id="mankind-w-paul-bearer-and-the-executioner-vs-freddie-joe-floyd" class="wp-block-heading">Mankind w/ Paul Bearer and The Executioner vs. Freddie Joe Floyd</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Tim White</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The bell rings, and they lock up before Mankind powers him to the corner. Floyd ducks a punch and starts punching the deranged Mankind. Mankind reverses a whip, but Floyd catches him with a dropkick. Mankind bounces into the ropes and big boots Floyd down before delivering a forearm to the head. A very spaced-out Executioner is shown watching the match. Floyd peppers Mankind with some backhands, followed by a jumping heel kick. Floyd charges, but Mankind ducks down to send him out of the ring. As Floyd gets into the ring, Mankind drops a leg across the bottom rope. Mankind chokes him on the middle rope before hitting a flurry of forearms. Mankind sends Floyd to the corner before hitting a running knee to the face. The Executioner’s eyes look glazed over. Mankind goes outside to further attack Floyd. Mankind gets in the ring and headbutts him before hitting a vicious pulling piledriver! Mankind looks at his fingers and gets psyched up before applying the Mandible Claw to Floyd.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-technical-submission-mankind" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Technical Submission: Mankind</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Paul Bearer steps into the ring and hands Mankind the urn as soft piano music plays. The Executioner stays outside and doesn’t look well.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>The bell tolls.</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Thunder rumbles in the arena, and the lights flicker. A shark cage with an effigy of Paul Bearer hanging upside down lowers from the ceiling. The Undertaker’s voice is heard over the speakers. Undertaker says, “Creatures of the Night… say hello to these poor, foolish souls, for this Sunday a new age of darkness begins. At the Survivor Series, the only thing to expect is the unexpected. As for you, Mr. Bearer, you take a long, hard look, for this is only the beginning of your torment and your torture. You poor unfortunate souls will never Rest in Peace.” Mankind and Paul Bearer look terrified. Mankind drives himself into the cage twice. The Executioner is unbothered. Mankind rips his hair out and screams.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706805 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> A simple and effective squash match for Mankind, who has made Freddie Joe Floyd a victim before. As for the post-match angle with The Undertaker having the effigy of Paul Bearer lowered… they literally showed the exact same angle happening on a WWF Live Event last week on Raw. There isn’t much left to do with Mankind and The Undertaker, so the build to this Survivor Series match has been a little lackluster.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> This year’s inductees into the WWF Hall of Fame include <strong>Walter “Killer” Kowalski</strong> and <strong>“Handsome” Jimmy and “Luscious” Johnny Valiant</strong>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
<iframe loading="lazy" title="Meet Rocky Maivia: Raw, Nov. 11, 1996" width="1200" height="675" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/qJq7eF2psng?feature=oembed" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</div></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Rocky Maivia is shown weightlifting and training in the ring. He is the first third-generational superstar in WWF history. Maivia says he has goosebumps thinking about it. It will be a feeling like no other, and he can’t wait. What’s got him so pumped up? His World Wrestling Federation debut. Kevin Kelly walks with him and asks what it will feel like to walk through the curtain, enter the hallowed halls of Madison Square Garden, and see the squared circle. Maivia says, “And I tell you it’s a feeling of an elation right now. I’m so jacked right now. I mean I could do it right now.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">MSG is where his grandfather and father made their names. When he sees 23,000 people screaming his name, he thinks it’s the same people who screamed his grandfather’s name 20 years ago and his father’s 10 years ago. It’s going to be an intense feeling. Footage shows Maivia recently introducing his father into the prestigious Cauliflower Alley Club in Tampa, Florida. Maivia said it was one of the greatest days of his life. Johnson says the key advice his father gave him was to always give 100%. The respect is given when it’s earned, so he’ll earn it. Maivia wants to get by not on his heritage, but by his unique wrestling ability. Maivia says, “Five years from now, when I look back, I just want to ask myself, ‘Did you give 110% effort with whatever you did and, if you did, did you become the best at it?’ Whatever it is, I just want to be able to say I was 110% committed and I gave 110% effort, and the fruits of my labor were plentiful.”</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706812 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> This was a phenomenal video package for the soon-to-debut Rocky Maivia, billed as the first third-generation wrestler in WWF history. That distinction is a little shaky. Jeff Jarrett had already worked for the WWF, and his lineage included his father, wrestler and promoter Jerry Jarrett, as well as wrestling/promotional ties through Eddie Marlin and Christine “Teeny” Jarrett, his paternal and maternal grandparents, respectively. The company never pushed that on television, though, and with Jarrett gone after a messy contract dispute, there was certainly no reason for the WWF to go out of its way to credit him here.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706813 wp-block-paragraph">Maivia is definitely green on interviews. The first time we hear from him, his first three sentences end with “right now.” Still, he’s got a great look, they did an incredible job of promoting his lineage and picked some great training footage of him to make him look really exciting. It’s interesting that they seem to be going with pushing him as a 1980s white meat babyface the week after they do the “Pillman’s Got a Gun” angle. As the company pushes into a more edgy style, will this kind of push work? We’ll find out.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706815 wp-block-paragraph">Also, I have a feeling that Mr. Maivia will be very pleased when he looks back at the five-year challenge he set for himself.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kevin Kelly is in the arena hallway, and Sycho Sid is pacing in the background. Sid knocks over a garbage can. Kelly says he’ll have an interview with Sid next.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> The WWF’s Big Bang Boom Tour is coming to an arena near you. This Sunday, the WWF takes over Madison Square Garden in New York City for the 1996 Survivor Series. One week from today, they’ll be at the New Haven Coliseum in New Haven, Connecticut for Monday Night Raw. Next Tuesday, they’ll be at the Springfield Civic Center in Springfield, Massachusetts. On Wednesday, they’ll be at the Westchester County Center in White Plains, New York. Thursday, they head north into Canada to the Robert Guertin Arena in Hull, Quebec. On Friday, they close out the tour at the Molson Centre in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. The WWF is exploding with action, and you can be at Ground Zero with the Big Bang Boom Tour.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kevin Kelly is backstage with an irate Sycho Sid. Kelly mentions that Shawn Michaels seemed to make an honest mistake earlier tonight. Sid tells Kelly to shut his mouth. In week one, it was him and Owen Hart, only for Michaels to come down. In week two, Sid returned the favor while Michaels was taking on The British Bulldog. In week three, there was a mistake. Sid was down on his face, and he turned to see Michaels holding a chair. Was it a mistake? He’ll give Michaels the benefit of the doubt. Sid says Michaels kicked him right in his face tonight. Mistake or not, there will be no mistake at Survivor Series. That night will be the end of the reign of Shawn Michaels. Michaels will step off the mountain, and the big man will step above. No man shall cast a stone to knock him down. Why? Because he is the master and the ruler of the world!</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706826 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> Super intense promo from Sycho Sid. Sid actually did a very good job of summing up everything that happened between them over the last four weeks before pivoting to Survivor Series.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Stone Cold” Steve Austin is standing backstage. Austin says all Vince McMahon seems to do is run his mouth when it’s Stone Cold’s time to talk. Bob Holly is going to get a wrestling lesson, but it won’t last long because it won’t take long for Austin to deal with him. Austin knows Bret Hart will be watching. Hart better watch because he needs to be prepared for Madison Square Garden. It is the most important match of Hart’s life. Hart is making a big comeback, trying to please the people and his family. Those are the things that will get Hart into trouble. Austin doesn’t have to take care of anyone but himself. Hart better keep watching. When he’s through with Bob Holly, he won’t wait for Madison Square Garden. If people thought last week was bad, he’ll find Hart’s dressing room. They don’t have to wait. The bottom line is that Stone Cold is the best there is! Austin walks off.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Milton Bradley Karate Fighters Holiday Tournament:</strong> Jerry “The King” Lawler and Todd Pettengill welcome us back to the tournament where Dok Hendrix and Sable will face off. Hendrix and Sable talk about their Karate Fighters. In the match, Sable defeated Hendrix. Lawler says, “Well, there’s Dok doing what Dok does best: begging a woman for something.” Next week, Mr. Bob Backlund battles Sunny.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Locker Room Interview</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kevin Kelly is backstage with WWF Champion Shawn Michaels. What went wrong tonight, and how does it relate to Survivor Series? Michaels says he and Sycho Sid have always had conflicting personalities. Sid wants to be called the master and ruler of the world. The Hitman wants to be a role model and a superhero. Everyone in the WWF wants to knock him and call him chump change, but everyone who has called him names has been kicked in the teeth by him at one time or another. It was a mistake what he did to Sid tonight, but he still knocked him out colder than a block of ice. What is Sid going to do this Sunday when Michaels is gunning for him? With the chin Sid is sporting, he can hit the kick from anywhere in Madison Square Garden. When he knocks Sid out, he’s going down to the WWF Champion whether he or any other WWF superstar likes it or not. Michaels wants Sid’s respect, and he’ll beat it out of him.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706838 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> That’s definitely the fieriest promo Shawn Michaels has done throughout this entire WWF Championship run. Most of the time, he’s done the “aw shucks, me?” type deal with the typical babyface, “I’m just doing my best” shtick. In my view, this is what this run needed more of in 1996. This is closer to what got Michaels over in the first place.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Jim Ross joins the commentary team for this match.</p>



<h3 id="bob-holly-vs-stone-cold-steve-austin" class="wp-block-heading">Bob Holly vs. “Stone Cold” Steve Austin</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Mike Chioda</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They circle the ring and lock up. Austin powers Holly into the corner and backs up before staring Holly down. They lock up, and Austin hooks a side headlock before hitting a takeover. Holly fights up and whips Austin off. Austin shoulder tackles Holly and lets him get back up. They lock up again, and Austin hooks a side headlock.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Bret “Hitman” Hart is shown watching the match in his locker room.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin hits a snapmare and applies a rear chin lock, but Holly fights out and stretches Austin on the mat. Austin fights up and elbows out before hitting a snapmare. Holly avoids an elbow drop and wrenches his arm, but Austin pokes him in the eyes. Austin chops Holly’s chest and bounces him off the top turnbuckle before hitting a headlock takeover for a two-count. Austin cinches in the hold, but Holly soon fights up and whips him off. Austin shoulder-tackles him, then sends him into the ropes and hits a Stun Gun. Austin hits a snapmare before going to the second rope for a bombs away elbow for a two-count. Austin stomps Holly and drops a knee on him. Austin rakes his boot across Holly’s face before bouncing him off the top turnbuckle twice. Austin whips Holly hard into the corner.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Hart is shown intently watching backstage. Vince McMahon announces that Austin will face Vader next week on Raw. Austin sends him into the ropes, but Holly takes him down. Holly goes for a running splash, but Austin gets his knees up.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Another Survivor Series promo video with “Stone Cold” Steve Austin. Austin says, “Pink tights… what the hell is that all about, Bret?” This isn’t ballet class. Austin says it’s all a load of crap. Hart is coming back to continue a legacy, but Austin will make his comeback a living hell. Austin thinks Hart is completely pathetic. Austin is the best there is. Austin 3:16 rules. Hart will beg for mercy at Madison Square Garden.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> On Saturday, November 16, the WWF Hall of Fame Banquet takes place at the Marriott in Times Square. On November 17, it’ll be the Survivor Series at Madison Square Garden.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Holly punches Austin away and shoulder tackles him. Austin soon floors him with a Lou Thesz Press before punching away at him. Holly quickly counters with a roll-up for a two-count. Austin pops up and hits a clothesline. Hart continues to watch the match from his locker room. Austin stomps Holly. Holly reverses a whip and hits a hurricanrana. Holly punches Austin and sends him into the ropes for a beautiful dropkick. 1… 2… Austin kicks out. Holly bounces Austin off the top turnbuckle. Austin reverses a whip and charges, but his shoulder hits the ring post when Holly moves. Holly goes to the top rope and dives, but Austin boots him in the face. Austin pulls Holly up and drops him with a Stone Cold Stunner for the win.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-pinfall-stone-cold-steve-austin" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Pinfall: “Stone Cold” Steve Austin</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin quickly leaves the ring as Bret Hart watches on. Replays of Austin’s victory over Holly are shown.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Stone Cold” Steve Austin walks up to Bret Hart’s locker room and pretends to go in, but stops himself. Austin says people would like him to beat up Hart for free, but he’s not going to do it. To see Stone Cold vs. The Hitman, you’re going to have to pay. Someone is stealing his lines, but this is the match of the decade. Austin knocks on the door and taunts Hart as the show comes to an end. Austin says the end of Bret Hart is coming, and that’s the bottom line!</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706867 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> In hindsight, this is another situation where the taped format hurts them. After the Brian Pillman angle last week, the last thing we need is to see Steve Austin have a two-segment match against Bob Holly. If this show could have been filmed in shorter spurts, they could have pivoted by adding another segment to the WWF Tag Title match earlier (maybe give us a better face comeback) and had Austin quickly run through Holly. Austin is a total superstar at this point, having completely emerged in just a few weeks. Holly is a going-nowhere guy with a going-nowhere gimmick. Just stun him and get it over with.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706869 wp-block-paragraph">In any event, Austin’s post-match foray into the backstage area to taunt Bret Hart, only to say people will need to pay to see him fight Hart, was well done. After the insanity of last week, this was a much more focused show, pointing toward the pay-per-view rather than trying to pop a shock-value rating.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706871 wp-block-paragraph">Also, my goodness, those Steve Austin video packages in the warehouse are among the best the WWF has ever produced. I try not to include any non-WWE-branded videos in this recap because you never know when they’ll issue a copyright strike against an account, leading to a dead link, but you really have to check out the video below.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
<iframe loading="lazy" title="Stone Cold Steve Austin 1996 Survivor Series Promo" width="1200" height="675" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/--AqiI545Ps?feature=oembed" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</div></figure>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h3 id="quick-match-results" class="wp-block-heading">Quick Match Results</h3>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706877 wp-block-paragraph">&#8212; Owen Hart and The British Bulldog* (c’s) def. Sycho Sid* and WWF Champion Shawn Michaels to retain the WWF Tag Team Championship<br>&#8212; Mankind def. Freddie Joe Floyd via Technical Submission<br>&#8212; “Stone Cold” Steve Austin def. Bob Holly</p>



<h3 id="mikes-wrap-up-what-we-learned-from-raw" class="wp-block-heading">Mike’s Wrap-Up: What We Learned from Raw</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After last week’s infamous chaos, this was a much more focused go-home episode for Survivor Series. The WWF apologized just enough for “Pillman’s Got a Gun,” kept “Stone Cold” Steve Austin dangerous by having him refuse to apologize, and then spent the rest of the night pushing the major matches for Madison Square Garden.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best build remains Austin vs. Bret “Hitman” Hart. Austin feels like a full-blown star now, and every promo he cuts makes Survivor Series feel bigger. The WWF Championship program with Shawn Michaels and Sycho Sid also got a solid final push. The tag title match was good, the accidental Sweet Chin Music finish worked, and both men finally showed some real fire heading into Sunday.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There was also some business news hanging over the show. According to the Wrestling Observer, Curt Hennig may have been on his way out of the WWF, with WCW reportedly interested and Titan already warning WCW about interfering with his contract. That makes the Mr. Perfect situation especially interesting, since he is still aligned with Hunter Hearst Helmsley on television and still being advertised for Survivor Series.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There were also reports that the WWF had suspended group drug testing while retaining the right to test individuals, a larger backstage story worth revisiting later as this series continues looking at the toll the Monday Night War took on the people involved.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rocky Maivia also received a strong introductory video package before his official debut at Survivor Series. It was a classic white-meat babyface presentation at a time when Raw is clearly getting darker and edgier, which makes his rollout fascinating to watch in context.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WCW still won the night, making it twenty straight weeks for Nitro. Raw’s number improved again in the earlier time slot, but Nitro remains far ahead. Creatively, though, the WWF has done a much better job over the last few weeks of making Survivor Series feel important. Now they need the pay-per-view to deliver.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706888 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Raw (USA Network, taped): 2.53 (Record: 16-36)</strong><br><strong>WCW Nitro (TNT, live): 3.53 (Record: 36-16)</strong></p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1794548706890 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Winner: WCW Nitro (20-week streak)</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Email – <a href="mailto:mike@wrestleview.com">mike@wrestleview.com</a><br>X – <a href="https://x.com/MikeTedescoWV">@MikeTedescoWV</a></p>



